// StartEmbedSessionWithBodyWithResponse Start an embed avatar session (API-key authenticated, LiveKit only)
//
// Starts a session on behalf of an embed API key instead of a signed-in
// user. Called by the customer's own backend — never a browser —
// with `X-Api-Key` in place of a JWT. Always LiveKit; there is no
// `framework` field. Responds 201 only once the avatar has actually
// joined the room, and returns `serverUrl`/`roomName` as explicit
// fields (not a combined `roomUrl`), since that's what
// `livekit-client`'s `Room.connect()` needs directly.
//
// **Length.** `maxDurationSeconds` is capped server-side at the
// smallest of the key's own `maxSessionSeconds`, the plan's
// `limits.maxSessionSeconds` (`GET /v1/account`) and, for `reserve`
// billing, what the balance can pay for. The response reports what you
// asked for (`requestedDurationSeconds`), what you got
// (`actualDurationSeconds`) and whether it was cut (`durationCapped`).
// The granted length counts from the moment both the visitor and the
// avatar are in the room, which is also when billing starts.
//
// **Joining and ending.** The visitor must join within 60 seconds of
// the avatar; otherwise the session ends `failed` with `endReason:
// no_user_joined` and nothing is billed. End it early with `POST
// /v1/session/{id}/end`; read its state, end reason and cost with `GET
// /v1/avatar_sessions/{id}`. An avatar renders one live conversation at
// a time: a second start on an avatar that is already in a live
// session of the account answers `409 avatar_busy`.
//
// **App tools.** `appTools.tools` declares functions the avatar may
// call during the conversation, with `appTools.instructions` saying
// how and when to use them. Every call reaches the visitor's LiveKit
// client as an RPC (method `atmee.tool`, payload `{id, name,
// arguments}`): register a handler for it on the room's local
// participant; with `x-atmee.mode` `await` its answer is the result
// the avatar is given. Tools run on Causeur, the conversation runtime
// v2 avatars are moving to. A session the behavior module runs (every v1 avatar, and a v2
// avatar not yet moved) accepts them and never calls them: which
// runtime serves a session is decided at launch, not by the request,
// so the start cannot refuse them. A rule broken answers
// `400 invalid_tools`, the message naming it; so does a tool set that
// would push the session over Causeur's payload limit with the
// avatar's own config. The request body may be up to 128 KiB.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /v1/session (the `StartEmbedSession` operationId).
//
// Deprecated: this operation has been marked as deprecated upstream, but no `x-deprecated-reason` was set
func (c *ClientWithResponses) StartEmbedSessionWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*StartEmbedSessionResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.StartEmbedSessionWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseStartEmbedSessionResponse(rsp)
}

